 ## Overview
 
-Obsession is a Victorian-era board game where players renovate country estates and improve their family's reputation by hosting events, inviting guests, hiring servants, and purchasing improvements. The game is played over 16 rounds (standard) or 20 rounds (extended) divided into 4 seasons, each ending with a courtship round. Reputation, prestige, and victory points determine the winner.
+Lead a Derbyshire family, restore its estate, host gentry and compete for the Fairchilds’ favour. Most total victory points (VP) wins.
+
+**Scope:** Dan Hallagan/Kayenta Games, Obsession second-edition **base/core game, 2–4 players, standard 16-round multiplayer**, using the publisher’s **Universal Rules Compendium (2025-12-17) plus Corrigendum 1.0 (2026-10-06)**. This source replaces the earlier third-party condensed 2.2E reference. The 16 spaces comprise four seasons of three ordinary rounds plus one courtship: **each player takes twelve ordinary turns**. Courtship is an event, with no player turns, service rotation, monument income or Servants’ Hall use.
+
+Use base family, tile, guest and objective components. Excluded: Upstairs, Downstairs; Characters; Wessex; promotional/backer tiles and guests; supplemental and second chance servants; replacement objective decks; expanded drafts; extended game; 5–6 players; solo/AI/estate challenge; tableau, teams and co-op; friendly mode; low VP monuments; milestones; distinguished family; alternate Charles; Costume Ball; starting hall boy; intensive service; closed courtship; Queen Victoria market; Charles Dickens courtship; and other optional variants, including **hiring when passing**. The **full seasonal market refresh is standard** in this compendium, as confirmed by the corrigendum; it is no longer merely the former Emily Brontë variant. The older Seasonal Flowchart player aid omits this refresh.
 
 ## Components
 
-- Player boards (family estates), Alderley Hall board (round/courtship track)
-- Derbyshire board (builders' market, servants for hire, guest decks)
-- Improvement tiles (rooms/spaces), Gentry cards (family + guests), Servant meeples
-- VP cards, Theme cards, Objective cards, Coins, Reputation counters
+Base game: four family boards and tableau organisers, main board, Alderley Hall/round-track board, first-player and round markers, four reminder tokens, reputation counters/markers, money, draw bag, improvement tiles, gentry, ten theme cards, thirty VP cards, thirty base objective cards and scorepad. The five tile categories are Essentials (brown), Service (blue), Estate (red), Prestige (purple) and Sporting (green). Common servants are footmen, valets and lady’s maids; unique servants are butler, housekeeper and underbutler. Printed tile/card requirements, favours, prices and VP govern; front and back can differ.
 
 ## Setup
 
-1. Place Alderley Hall board (16 or 20 round side up). Place white pawn on round 1.
-2. Shuffle VP cards and theme cards; stack face down on board spaces.
-3. Each player takes a family with: player board, 5 starting tiles, reputation counters (start at 1), 5 starting servants, 4 family member cards, 2 starting guest cards.
-4. On Derbyshire board: shuffle casual and prestige guest decks, place servants for hire, fill Builders' Market with allowed tiles.
-5. Deal 5 objective cards per player.
+1. Give a random player the first-player marker. **Starting to that player’s left and going clockwise**, each chooses an available base family board. Leave room for five tableau columns, using the board’s printed organiser or a separate organiser.
+2. Set reputation to **level 1, position 1**. Stack the 1/2, 3/4, 5/6 and Max counters with 1 showing, Max at the bottom. Do not use 7/8 counters in standard play.
+3. Put one butler, housekeeper, valet, lady’s maid and footman in each player’s **Available Service**. Give each the five starting tiles—Private Study, Butler’s Room, Main Gazebo, Front Parlour and Bowling Green—with the starter/building icon showing, and their four family cards (Lord, Lady, Heir, Young Lady). Return unused starters/families to the box.
+4. Separate starter guests from the casual deck, shuffle them and deal two to each player’s hand. **Shuffle every unused starter back into the casual deck.** Separately shuffle the casual and prestige decks onto the main board. Deal each player five secret base objectives; put the remaining shuffled deck on the board.
+5. Apply family bonus: **Asquith** adds its Dowager Countess (a fifth family card); **Cavendish** starts at reputation 1.4; **Ponsonby** gains £300; **York** adds a footman to Available Service. Other families start with no money. Make a public money supply.
+6. After distributing starting servants, stock **Servants for Hire** with **one valet and one lady’s maid per player, two footmen per player, and two underbutlers total**. No butlers or housekeepers. Return unused servants to the box: they are unavailable this game.
+7. Select **one double-sided monument per player plus one**, choosing at most one of Sculpture Garden and Garden Maze. The other choices are Imported Marble Floor, Largest Wine Cellar, Manor Gargoyles and Big Game Trophy Room. Return unselected double-sided monuments to the box. Put selected monuments and **all nonstarter base improvement tiles** in the draw bag; tiles that later upgrade into monuments always go in the bag and do not count against this setup selection.
+8. Draw until the Builders’ Market has **six different eligible tiles**: PR3 or less (including no PR), not Servants’ Hall, and not a double-sided monument. Set ineligible draws aside and return them to the bag after filling. Stack duplicate eligible tiles in the same space. Place normal tiles with the upgrade/rose icon hidden; hybrids start with the **underlined hybrid icon** showing. Sort the six spaces by ascending printed sorting number, beginning at the £300 space.
+9. Put Alderley Hall standard side up with round marker at 1 and one reminder token per player above the track. Shuffle theme and VP decks separately. Reveal the first theme (open courtship); keep revealed themes in order through the game. Place the base Charles and Elizabeth Fairchild cards face up on Alderley Hall.
 
 ## Turn Structure
 
-The game flows through 4 seasons on the round track. Each season has ordinary rounds and ends with a courtship round.
-
-### Ordinary Round
-1. Reveal a theme card at start of each season.
-2. Each player (clockwise) takes a **Standard Turn** or **Passes**.
-
-### Standard Turn
-1. Refresh servants (Servants' Quarters -> Available; Expended -> Servants' Quarters).
-2. **Host an Activity:** Choose an improvement tile with an activity. Your reputation must meet or exceed the tile's prestige.
-3. **Invite Guests:** Play required gentry cards from hand. Reputation must meet guest prestige.
-4. **Provide Service:** Assign available servants to tile and cards.
-5. **Enjoy Favors:** Gain money, reputation, new guests, VP cards, or other benefits from the activity.
-6. **Buy from Builders' Market:** Purchase 1 improvement tile (pay cost + any modifier).
-7. **Clear Board:** Return tile and servants; discard used guests.
-
-### Passing Turn
-- Move all servants to Available Service.
-- Recover discarded guests to active hand.
-- Gain £200 or free market refresh.
-- May still buy from Builders' Market.
-
-### Courtship Round
-Compare VP totals in the current theme category. Sole winner takes a Fairchild card. Each winner draws 1 VP card. Discard 1 objective card. Pass first-player pawn.
+Each ordinary round, the first player takes one turn, then each other player clockwise. Choose **host an activity or pass**. After the last player’s turn, advance the marker one space. Resolve courtship when reached; after the first three courtships prepare the next season.
+
+### Hosting an activity
+
+1. **Rotate service:** move servants in Servants’ Quarters to Available Service first, then those in Expended Service to Servants’ Quarters. Each advances exactly one space; do not move expended servants twice. Unused available servants remain available.
+2. **Check round track, then ongoing tiles:** resolve the round event first. Then resolve applicable monument and Servants’ Hall effects in any order. These also happen when passing, after servants and gentry return.
+3. **Choose activity:** select one tile from your tableau with an activity and put it on your family board’s Activity space. Ongoing-effect tiles cannot host activities.
+4. **Invite gentry:** play from hand the **exact number and types** printed on the activity. “Gentry” includes any family or guest. **Ladies includes every female family member or female guest; Gentlemen includes every male family member or male guest.** Thus female family members can fill ladies requirements, and male family members can fill gentlemen requirements. Casual/prestige requirements instead require that guest type and exclude family; family requirements accept only family members. You cannot exceed or fall short of the printed requirement. The Butler’s Room has no gentry. Each played guest and the activity tile must meet the reputation screening rule below.
+5. **Provide service:** put an available servant of each required type on every tile/card service icon. Each servant can provide one service, with permitted substitutions. Without all required gentry and service, choose another legal activity or pass; if no activity is possible, you must pass. The Servants’ Quarters tile and Service Help offer the specified exceptions below.
+6. **Enjoy favours:** collect all tile and played-card favours. Resolve all **positive favours before all negative favours**, otherwise choosing their order. Favours are mandatory unless expressly optional (such as “may” or guest dismissal). New guest invites draw from the indicated deck, reveal the guest to everyone and add it to hand. Guests acquired here do not replace or enlarge the activity’s required attendees.
+7. **Buy:** optionally buy one improvement from the Market or Reserve. Buying is also allowed on a pass. Builders’ Holiday and Market Purchase VP cards allow multiple purchases as described below.
+8. **Clear board:** all servants used in any capacity go to **Expended Service**; all played gentry go to your **personal discard pile**. Return the activity tile to its correct tableau column, flipping/upgrading it as required. The last player advances the round marker.
+
+### Passing
+
+Follow this order: **all servants directly to Available Service → all gentry in your discard to hand → round-track event, then ongoing tile effects → choose £200 or a free Market refresh → optional one tile purchase → last player advances marker**. Servants used for an ongoing effect during this pass are expended at the end of the turn. Passing gives no activity favours and flips no activity tile. **Hiring with Butler’s Room is an activity in this base scope, not a passing benefit.**
+
+### Round-track events
+
+| Standard space | Event | Effect on each player’s turn |
+|---|---|---|
+| 3 and 9 | Village Fair | If your Private Study is already upgraded, immediately gain £300 and 2 reputation. No reward if it is not upgraded at this step. |
+| 6 | Draw Objectives | Draw two objectives; keep both secret with your other objectives. |
+| 11 | Builders’ Holiday | May buy any number of tiles from the current Market and/or Reserve, within your funds. |
+| 14 | National Holiday | Ignore PR on all tiles and guests when hosting; still obey all other requirements, including service and gentry counts/types. |
+| 4, 8, 12, 16 | Courtship | Only the courtship event; no ordinary turn or ongoing income. |
 
 ## Actions
 
-### Special Actions (anytime during turn)
-- Gain £100 at cost of 2 reputation increments (repeatable).
-- Refresh 1 servant at cost of 3 reputation increments (repeatable).
-- Refresh Builders' Market at cost of 4 reputation increments (once per turn).
-
-### Servant Types
-Butler, Housekeeper, Valet, Lady's Maid, Footman, Underbutler. Some substitutions allowed (e.g., Housekeeper for Lady's Maid).
+### Reputation, wealth and special actions
+
+Each reputation symbol is **one increment**, not one level. Move clockwise to gain and anticlockwise to lose. Passing position 5 to 1 raises the level; passing 1 to 5 lowers it. For arithmetic, level L and wheel position P represent **5 × (L − 1) + (P − 1)** increments above 1.1; Max means level 7 in standard play. A full level spans five increments. Minimum is **1.1**, maximum in standard play **7.5 (Max.5)**. Ignore reputation losses beyond minimum. At minimum you cannot be targeted by an opponent’s reputation-reducing effect, or take a special action. Negative-reputation guests can still be played; resolve positives first before applying losses down to the minimum.
+
+**Reputation screening is fixed at the end of step 2, before Choose Activity.** That level must meet or exceed the PR on both the chosen tile and every played guest. It remains the screening level for the whole activity: special actions during Choose Activity, Invite Gentry or later may lower actual reputation below those PRs without invalidating the activity. Reputation earned during Enjoy Favours does not retroactively qualify an activity or guest. National Holiday waives PR. Family and Fairchild cards have no PR; family also needs no personal service. You may **acquire and score** guests/tiles above your reputation: PR limits play, not acquisition or scoring.
+
+Special actions are available at any time on your own turn, hosting or passing, with sufficient reputation to pay:
+
+| Action | Cost and effect | Limit |
+|---|---|---|
+| Borrow Money | Lose 2 reputation increments; gain £100 | Repeatable |
+| Service Help | Lose 3 increments; move one servant **from Servants’ Quarters to Available Service** | Repeatable; cannot take a servant directly from Expended Service |
+| Refresh Market | Lose 4 increments; fully refresh the Market | This special action once per turn |
+
+At Max.5, excess reputation is lost unless spent immediately on special actions. You may take special actions before or even in the middle of resolving a reputation favour to create space. Other refresh methods (pass benefit and VP cards) do not use up the once-per-turn special refresh allowance. **You may use multiple Market Refresh VP cards in the same turn**, each giving a separate full refresh. You may combine those cards with one free passing-benefit refresh and one paid special-action refresh on that passing turn, subject to the purchase-timing restrictions below.
+
+Money held is public. Pay costs to the supply and take gains from it; never spend more money than you have. Money supply is unlimited (use proxies if needed). You may exchange denominations, but may not give or receive money between players in this mode.
+
+A money penalty must be fully payable. You may use the activity’s positive money favours, a money VP card or Borrow Money. If money is short, you must borrow if possible; if you cannot fund the payment, you cannot play that guest/tile. You cannot take a negative favour first to shelter it at minimum reputation.
+
+### Servants and hiring
+
+Butler serves tile/card butler needs. Housekeeper serves housekeeper needs and **may substitute for a lady’s maid only when no lady’s maids are available**. Valets and lady’s maids serve their printed needs. With a **Brushing Room** in your tableau, footmen **may substitute for valets only when no valets are available**. A servant already assigned is not available for another service.
+
+The **underbutler may substitute for any base male servant** (butler, valet, footman), even when that type remains available. Unique servants are limited to one of each type per player and cannot leave your employ. Common servants can be recruited; their supply is limited to those put into play at setup.
+
+**Butler’s Room:** host a Servant Hiring activity with an available butler (or underbutler) and **no gentry**. Take up to two servants from Servants for Hire, excluding underbutlers, into Expended Service. No money hiring fee is charged. Clear the activity normally: used butler expended, tile upgrades on first use. The upgraded room alternatively lets you **recruit one common servant** (footman, valet or lady’s maid) from **any location on one opponent’s family board** into your Expended Service, instead of hiring up to two from supply. You cannot recruit unique servants.
+
+**Butler’s Pantry:** purchasing it is the **only way to acquire an underbutler**; take one from the supply immediately into Expended Service. Neither Butler’s Room nor Hire Servant VP cards can hire an underbutler. **An opponent can never recruit your underbutler: he is a unique servant, not a common servant.** He is **not immediately available**: normally he moves from Expended Service to Servants’ Quarters at your next service rotation, then to Available Service at your **second subsequent rotation**. Passing or a Refresh Service VP card can make him available earlier; once in Quarters, Service Help or the Servants’ Quarters tile can permit earlier use.
+
+**Servants’ Quarters tile:** each turn you may use **one servant from the Servants’ Quarters area** in addition to Available Service. This is the tile’s ability, separate from the board area and from Service Help. After use the servant goes to Expended Service normally. Ongoing service abilities apply once their tiles are in your tableau and do not require hosting an activity on those tiles.
+
+### Gentry, screening, dismissal and family
+
+There is no hand or discard-pile limit. Played cards go to your personal discard; passing recovers them. Guests remain yours until dismissed; discard-pile gentry score normally. Family can **never be dismissed**. Starter guests are casual guests with a starter icon and otherwise follow normal casual rules. **Charles Fairchild is male and Elizabeth Fairchild is female.** Both are prestige guests with no PR; Charles still requires a valet and Elizabeth a lady’s maid. Each is worth 8 VP if retained from final courtship.
+
+For **screening guests**, draw the stated number, choose the stated number to keep, reveal only the kept cards and add them to hand. Put rejected cards on the bottom of their guest deck without revealing them. **Dismissal** is always optional: choose an eligible guest from **hand, current activity or discard** and put it on the bottom of its matching guest deck. It is no longer yours, returns neither on passing nor for your VP, and can be a guest just drawn by an earlier favour. Dismissal does not remove family. Casual guests with at least one red frame (negative favour or VP) are unsavoury; other casual guests are respectable. Prestige guests and family are separate categories.
+
+| Base family card | Favour | VP |
+|---|---|---:|
+| Lord | £200 | 3 |
+| Lady | Choose screen two casual guests/keep one, **or** optionally dismiss one guest | 2 |
+| Heir | Choose £100 **or** one reputation | 2 |
+| Young Lady | Draw one casual guest; additionally gain two reputation if a **male prestige guest** attends the same activity | 1 |
+| Asquith Dowager | Both £100 **and** one reputation | 1 |
+
+Charles Fairchild gives three reputation. Elizabeth gives **both** one prestige guest and two reputation. The base gossips are Miss Elizabeth Perkins (reduce one opponent by one reputation) and Miss Florence Oliver (reduce one opponent by two, and lose one yourself); minimum-reputation protection still applies.
+
+### Improvements and ongoing effects
+
+Normal activity tiles **must upgrade after first use**: flip them to the rose/upgrade side at cleanup. An already-upgraded normal tile remains on that side on subsequent uses. Do not flip merely because you purchased a tile. Ongoing-only tiles cannot host activities or be upgraded; identical sides have the same effect. When acquiring an ongoing-effect tile, take a reminder token onto your family board.
+
+**Hybrids** have category icons instead of a rose: start with the underlined icon up, **flip after every use**, and move to the newly face-up category. Only that face-up category counts for courtship and objectives. Base hybrids are Green Room (Essentials/Prestige) and South Lawn (Sporting/Estate). South Lawn’s Estate side needs five gentry, including at least two family.
+
+**Monuments** give one reputation per ordinary turn during the ongoing-effects step, hosting or passing. No income during courtship; a newly purchased monument first pays on a later turn. The Queen’s Suite and Lionheart Suite each host their front-side activity once, upgrade into a monument, and can never host again; their monument benefit begins on subsequent turns.
+
+**Servants’ Hall:** during the ongoing-effects step (2 hosting, 3 passing), place any one available servant on it to decrease one opponent’s reputation by one and gain one yourself. You may still host an activity or pass normally. The servant cannot also serve the activity and goes to Expended Service at turn end. Do not target a player at minimum reputation.
+
+**Barn:** every time you host an **Estate** activity, gain one reputation. It gives no reputation just for owning an Estate tile or passing.
+
+| Suite activity | Tile service and guest | Favours and later state |
+|---|---|---|
+| Heritage Guest Suite | One footman on tile, one casual guest from hand; provide guest’s normal service | Double **all** guest favours, including negative ones. Upgrades and remains an activity tile. |
+| Queen’s Suite | Housekeeper on tile, one prestige guest from hand; provide normal guest service | Double **one chosen favour**; enjoy others normally. Upgrades into a monument. |
+| Lionheart Suite | Butler on tile, **draw one prestige guest from the deck** to attend; no personal guest service | Double nonnegative favours; negative favours are excluded from doubling. Keep the guest and place it in your discard at cleanup. Upgrades into a monument. |
+
+All three suites have a **fixed count of one guest**; no effect may increase or decrease it. Base cards have no socialite/antisocialite attendee modifiers; those and their special redraw rules belong to excluded expansions.
+
+**Private Study** hosts two family with a butler, has no tile favour, then upgrades permanently into an ongoing tile. Its upgraded side gives £300 and two reputation **on Village Fair rounds only**, without staffing or hosting it again. The starting tile’s mere possession does not earn that reward.
+
+**Main Library:** unupgraded activity uses a butler and two family to draw **one extra objective**; after upgrading it uses a butler and three family to draw **one VP card**. Extra objectives increase the number you can retain for final scoring.
+
+Other base activity tiles follow their printed counts, service and favours on the current side. These include Bowling Green, Main Gazebo, Front Parlour; Billiards Room, Breakfast Room, Cabinet of Curiosities, Cricket Field, Croquet Lawn, Drawing Room, English Garden, Fenced Paddock, Flower Room, French Garden, Gabled Conservatory, Great Hall, Hillside Kennels, Long Gallery, Music Room, North Dining Room, North Library, Riding Stables, Smoking Room, State Room, Tennis Court and West Saloon. For example, use the exact larger gentry count when an upgraded tile changes it; Long Gallery instead reduces from four to two. Base **West Terrace** remains an activity on both sides. **Both unupgraded and upgraded sides require one butler and exactly three family cards.** The unupgraded tile favour is **draw one prestige guest**. Its upgraded tile favours are **draw one prestige guest AND optionally dismiss one to four guests** from hand, current activity or discard to the bottom of their appropriate decks. The base tile never becomes an ongoing dismissal tile; that is the excluded alternate West Terrace. Follow screening draw/keep quantities on Fenced Paddock, Gabled Conservatory and other tiles; optional dismissals may include a guest just drawn.
+
+### Builders’ Market and Reserve
+
+Normally buy **one tile total** per ordinary turn, from either Market or Reserve, after favours or your pass benefit. Pay the market-space price **plus the tile’s printed price modifier**. Reserve base price is **£300**, also with the modifier. A tile’s price cannot fall below **£100**. Place the bought tile in its tableau category with the same side showing. **Never acquire a duplicate tile you already own, by any means**; otherwise reputation does not restrict purchasing.
+
+**Refill:** if a Market space became empty, slide remaining tiles left. Draw replacements into the rightmost spaces. Stack any drawn duplicate on its matching tile already in the Market and draw again; duplicate tiles occupy one space and share that space’s price. Buying only one tile from a two-tile stack leaves an occupied space and causes no refill. Normal tiles enter with upgrade icon hidden, hybrids with underlined hybrid icon showing. If filling multiple spaces, sort **only the new tiles** by ascending sorting number. Qualifying Reserve tiles go straight to Reserve and you draw again.
+
+**Open Reserve:** after courtship 1, move all Market **Service tiles except Largest Wine Cellar** to Reserve. From then on newly drawn Service tiles go directly there, still excepting Largest Wine Cellar. After courtship 2, also move all Market **PR1** tiles there; all later PR1 draws also go directly there. Largest Wine Cellar is a monument that **never** enters Reserve. **Every Reserve tile is public and available for selection. You may inspect all tiles at any time and buy any one of them, including a tile underneath another in a stack; there is no top-tile-only restriction.** Normal price, duplicate and purchase limits still apply.
+
+**Refresh:** set **all Market tiles** aside, leave the Reserve unchanged, refill all six spaces from the bag under normal refill rules (sort by number), **then** return the set-aside tiles to the bag and mix. They cannot reappear in that same refresh. Full refresh occurs after **each of the first three courtships**, after Reserve movement, and can also be obtained via pass benefit, special action or VP card.
+
+**Builders’ Holiday / Market Purchase VP card:** may buy any number of tiles from Market and/or Reserve as funds allow, but **finish all purchases before sliding/refilling**. Buy from a **single unchanged market** at its existing prices. **Each tile keeps the Market-space price (or Reserve price) plus its printed modifier that applied before the first purchase in this buying sequence.** Buying an earlier tile does not slide other tiles into cheaper spaces or change their prices during the sequence. Once you have bought a tile on Builders’ Holiday, you may not refresh between purchases with a special action or VP card. **The single-unchanged-market restriction, including no refresh between purchases, applies to both Builders’ Holiday and a Market Purchase VP-card benefit.** Refill all resulting empty spaces together after buying finishes.
+
+### Objectives and VP cards
+
+Keep objectives secret until end scoring. Normally receive five at setup plus two on round 6; discard one at **each** of four courtships, leaving **three to score**. Additional objectives from Main Library or a VP card add to the final retained count; they do not cause an extra discard. Put discards face down on the bottom of the objective deck; shuffle simultaneous discards together first. A tile objective requires owning the named tile(s) at game end, **not upgrading them**, even if its illustration shows roses.
+
+A **VP card** offers a choice: its printed VP if retained, or its printed benefit if revealed and discarded. You may use it immediately on drawing it, at any time on your own later turn (several cards allowed), and once more after final courtship before scoring. Used VP cards go to the **bottom of the VP deck**; retained ones stay hidden with objectives. You cannot take both a card’s benefit and its final VP.
+
+| VP-card benefit | Resolution |
+|---|---|
+| Reputation / Money | Gain the printed two/three reputation or £200/£300. |
+| Market Purchase | Buy any number under the Builders’ Holiday rules. |
+| Market Refresh | Fully refresh Market under normal refresh rules. |
+| Objective | Draw two objectives, keep one, return the other to bottom of objective deck. This gives one additional retained objective. |
+| Refresh Service | Move **all servants directly to Available Service**; usable immediately. |
+| Prestige Guest | Draw one prestige guest, reveal and add to hand. |
+| Hire Servant | Take one servant from Servants for Hire, **not underbutler**, into Expended Service. |
+| Refresh Guest | Return one gentry of choice **including a family member** from personal discard to hand; usable immediately. If returned **before the Invite Gentry step**, you may choose that returned card to attend this turn’s activity, subject to its normal requirements. Returning to hand does not automatically play the card, but you may then play it during attendee selection. |
 
 ## Scoring / Victory Conditions
 
-At game end, sum:
-- VP on improvement tiles
-- VP on gentry cards
-- VP for completed objective cards
-- VP for reputation level
-- 2 VP per servant
-- 1 VP per £200
-- VP from retained VP cards
-
-**Highest total wins.** Tiebreaker: Fairchild card holder, then reputation.
+### Courtship and seasonal upkeep
+
+At each courtship, **first return both Fairchilds to Alderley Hall**, wherever the previous winner holds the card. In courtships 1–3, add the current face-up theme category’s tile VP, including negative values; only the currently face-up hybrid category counts. Highest theme score wins one VP card and chooses **one Fairchild to add to hand**. If tied for highest, **each tied player draws one VP card and no one receives a Fairchild**. Every player then discards one secret objective; shuffle those discards and bottom-deck them.
+
+After courtships 1–3, follow this order: **open the relevant Reserve → fully refresh Market → reveal the next theme to the right of previous themes → pass first-player marker anticlockwise → advance round marker**. Refresh is required by the selected standard rules even if nothing moved to Reserve; no seasonal upkeep occurs after courtship 4.
+
+**Final courtship (round 16):** return Fairchilds as usual, then total tile VP for **all four revealed theme cards**, rather than only the latest. A category shown twice counts twice, including its negative VP. Highest score gets one VP card and a Fairchild. On a tie, highest **reputation level** breaks it; wheel position is ignored. If still tied, no Fairchild is awarded, and the tied winners draw VP cards under the courtship tie rule. Discard the final objective. Then players have a final chance to use special actions and VP cards for their benefits where possible. Move the round marker to Wedding Bells and score.
+
+Fairchilds otherwise behave as prestige guests for play, discard and recovery. Only the player who wins a Fairchild in **final courtship** retains its 8 VP for final scoring; previous-season possession gives no permanent card VP.
+
+### Final VP
+
+| Source | Scoring |
+|---|---|
+| Improvement tiles | All printed face-up VP, including negatives and unused tiles. |
+| Gentry | All printed VP in hand **and discard**, including negatives, family and the final Fairchild. Dismissed guests do not score. |
+| Objectives | Score every remaining objective fulfilled at game end. |
+| Reputation | Levels **1 / 2 / 3 / 4 / 5 / 6 / 7 (Max)** score **1 / 3 / 6 / 10 / 15 / 21 / 28 VP**. Wheel position has no effect. |
+| Servants | **2 VP for every servant** in your employ, all types and locations. |
+| Wealth | **1 VP per £200**, rounded down. |
+| VP cards | Printed VP on every retained, unused VP card. |
+
+Highest total wins. Ties: tied player with final Fairchild wins; otherwise highest reputation **level** wins; if still tied share victory. Reputation wheel position never breaks a final courtship or game tie.
+
+### Base objective reference
+
+Score these base-deck objectives only when held. A category bonus counts monuments and hybrids currently in that category. “Guest” excludes family; prestige guests include a final Fairchild. The **basic-servant** objective counts **only common footmen, valets and lady’s maids**, unlike ordinary service VP which counts all servants.
+
+| Objective | Points / requirement |
+|---|---|
+| Service Group | 6 VP: Barn and Brushing Room |
+| Epicurean Group | 9 VP: Breakfast Room and North Dining Room |
+| Prestige Group | 10 VP: Smoking Room and Drawing Room |
+| Nature Group | 11 VP: Flower Room and any one of English Garden, French Garden, Sculpture Garden, Garden Maze |
+| Animal Lover’s Group | 12 VP: Fenced Paddock, Hillside Kennels and Riding Stables |
+| Domestic Group | 12 VP: Gabled Conservatory and either Main Library or North Library |
+| Literature Group | 12 VP: Main Library and North Library |
+| Sporting Group | 13 VP: Tennis Court, Cricket Field and Croquet Lawn |
+| Ladies’ Group | 16 VP: Gabled Conservatory, Flower Room and Music Room |
+| Gentlemen’s Group | 17 VP: Billiards Room, Hillside Kennels and Cabinet of Curiosities |
+| Guest bonuses | 1 VP per prestige guest; or 1 VP per complete three guests (all nonfamily guests) |
+| Category bonuses | 1 VP per tile in the specified Essentials, Service, Estate, Prestige or Sporting category |
+| Basic servants | 1 VP per common servant; exclude butler, housekeeper and underbutler |
+| Servant sets | 5 VP for two valets and two lady’s maids; 5 VP for underbutler; 6 VP for three lady’s maids; 6 VP for three valets; 7 VP for at least ten servants total |
+| Individual tiles | 5 VP for the named English Garden, Cabinet of Curiosities, French Garden or State Room |
+| Reputation | 7 VP for maximum **level** (7), regardless of wheel position |
+| Monuments | 3 VP per monument, including upgraded Queen’s Suite and Lionheart Suite |
+| Wealth | 10 VP for at least £1200 |
 
 ## Special Rules & Edge Cases
 
-- **Reputation system:** Main level (1-Max) with incremental marker (1-5). Increments cycle to raise main level. Never goes below 1.
-- **Black rose tiles:** Flip once when first used; show different side after.
-- **Hybrid (white flower) tiles:** Flip each time used; change category.
-- **Monuments:** Provide reputation each turn at start.
-- **Servants' Hall:** Allows gossip action (reduce opponent reputation, gain yours).
-- **Fairchild guests:** Prestigious temporary guests won during courtships.
-- **Market reserves:** Service tiles and prestige-1 tiles move to reserves after courtships 1 and 2.
+- Reputation gained in step 2 affects activity screening; reputation changes **after** that screening do not alter which tile/guests can be played this turn. Special-action payments must still be affordable.
+- Borrow Money and Service Help may repeat; the special-action Market refresh is once per turn. A free pass refresh and VP refreshes have separate availability.
+- A negative money favour cannot be ignored for being penniless. A negative reputation favour cannot take reputation below 1.1.
+- Upgrading is mandatory; buying alone does not upgrade. Hybrids keep alternating; suites that become monuments have only one activity use.
+- Full seasonal Market refresh is part of this source’s base standard flow. The corrigendum specifically directs players to compendium page 23 instead of the incomplete older player aid.
 
